1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">Graph neural networks power everything from
citation search to recommendation,</font>

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:10,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they are alarmingly easy to fool by injecting a
few malicious nodes into the graph.</font>

3
00:00:11,035 --> 00:00:16,127
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, from the Hong Kong Polytechnic
University and published at ICML 2024,</font>

4
00:00:16,207 --> 00:00:21,873
<font color="#FFFFFF">presents the first collective certified robustness
scheme against graph injection attacks.</font>

5
00:00:21,953 --> 00:00:26,854
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of certifying each node in isolation, which
is hopelessly pessimistic,</font>

6
00:00:26,934 --> 00:00:32,281
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors certify a whole set of target nodes at
once by formulating the worst-case</font>

7
00:00:32,361 --> 00:00:34,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">attacker as an optimization problem,</font>

8
00:00:34,659 --> 00:00:40,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">then relaxing that hard integer program into a
linear program that solves in about a minute.</font>

9
00:00:40,534 --> 00:00:42,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">The payoff is dramatic: on Citeseer,</font>

10
00:00:42,832 --> 00:00:47,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">the certified ratio jumps from zero percent to over
eighty percent.</font>

11
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:51,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">Graph neural networks are the workhorses of graph
learning,</font>

12
00:00:51,144 --> 00:00:57,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they can be broken by a graph injection attack
that slips a handful of malicious nodes into the graph.</font>

13
00:00:57,853 --> 00:01:01,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">To trust these models we want certified robustness,</font>

14
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:05,241
<font color="#FFFFFF">a mathematical guarantee that predictions stay
stable under attack.</font>

15
00:01:05,321 --> 00:01:10,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that every existing certificate for
injection attacks works node by node,</font>

16
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:13,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">certifying each target in isolation.</font>

17
00:01:13,169 --> 00:01:15,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">That sample-wise view is far too pessimistic,</font>

18
00:01:16,017 --> 00:01:21,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in practice it certifies almost nothing once
the attacker gets a modest budget.</font>

19
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:25,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is the key insight.</font>

20
00:01:25,153 --> 00:01:28,656
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the real world,</font>

21
00:01:28,736 --> 00:01:32,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">an attacker cannot conjure a different graph for
every node they want to fool.</font>

22
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:35,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">They inject one perturbed graph,</font>

23
00:01:35,902 --> 00:01:39,405
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that single graph has to disrupt the entire set
of target nodes at the same time.</font>

24
00:01:39,485 --> 00:01:42,988
<font color="#FFFFFF">If we certify the whole target set jointly instead
of one node at a time,</font>

25
00:01:43,068 --> 00:01:46,571
<font color="#FFFFFF">the guarantee should be much stronger.</font>

26
00:01:46,651 --> 00:01:50,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior collective methods existed for
edge-modification attacks,</font>

27
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:53,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they assume a fixed receptive field and simply
do not carry over to injection attacks,</font>

28
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:57,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">which expand the receptive field by adding new edges.</font>

29
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:03,087
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper delivers the first collective certified
robustness scheme for graph neural</font>

30
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:05,338
<font color="#FFFFFF">networks against injection attacks.</font>

31
00:02:05,418 --> 00:02:09,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors cast certification as a worst-case
optimization problem,</font>

32
00:02:09,792 --> 00:02:13,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">a binary integer quadratic constrained linear program,</font>

33
00:02:13,266 --> 00:02:19,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then introduce a customized linearization that
relaxes this hard program into an ordinary</font>

34
00:02:19,247 --> 00:02:22,126
<font color="#FFFFFF">linear program that can be solved efficiently.</font>

35
00:02:22,206 --> 00:02:26,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is almost model-agnostic: it works for
any message-passing GNN,</font>

36
00:02:26,966 --> 00:02:32,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it buys huge gains in certified performance at
very little computational cost.</font>

37
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:37,516
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method builds on randomized smoothing,
specifically node-aware bi-smoothing,</font>

38
00:02:37,596 --> 00:02:42,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">which randomly deletes edges and nodes to blur the
attacker's influence.</font>

39
00:02:42,147 --> 00:02:47,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">The crucial observation is locality: an injected
node can only harm a target if at least</font>

40
00:02:47,709 --> 00:02:51,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">one message-passing path between them survives the
smoothing.</font>

41
00:02:51,564 --> 00:02:55,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors upper-bound the probability of that
message interference,</font>

42
00:02:55,925 --> 00:02:58,121
<font color="#FFFFFF">turn it into a certifying condition,</font>

43
00:02:58,201 --> 00:03:03,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then model a worst-case attacker who tries to
make as many target nodes non-robust</font>

44
00:03:03,636 --> 00:03:07,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">as possible under a budget on injected nodes and edges.</font>

45
00:03:07,112 --> 00:03:11,077
<font color="#FFFFFF">That worst-case problem is the binary integer
quadratic program.</font>

46
00:03:11,157 --> 00:03:14,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because it is NP-hard, they relax it to a linear program,</font>

47
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,449
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they offer two versions.</font>

48
00:03:16,529 --> 00:03:19,673
<font color="#FFFFFF">The standard relaxation needs many extra variables,</font>

49
00:03:19,753 --> 00:03:23,212
<font color="#FFFFFF">while their customized reformulation, Collective-LP-two,</font>

50
00:03:23,292 --> 00:03:26,625
<font color="#FFFFFF">first collapses a quadratic term into a single vector,</font>

51
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:33,009
<font color="#FFFFFF">cutting the extra variables from order rho-squared
to order rho and improving both quality and speed.</font>

52
00:03:33,089 --> 00:03:37,749
<font color="#FFFFFF">Since the linear program has a larger feasible
region than the integer one,</font>

53
00:03:37,829 --> 00:03:42,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">its answer is always a sound lower bound on the
true certified ratio.</font>

54
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:46,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation uses two standard citation graphs,
Cora-ML and Citeseer,</font>

55
00:03:47,008 --> 00:03:51,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a few thousand nodes each, and two
representative backbones,</font>

56
00:03:51,145 --> 00:03:54,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">a graph convolution network and a graph attention network.</font>

57
00:03:54,835 --> 00:04:00,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">The attacker's budget is swept from twenty up to a
hundred and sixty injected nodes,</font>

58
00:04:00,181 --> 00:04:04,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the per-node edge limit set to each graph's
average degree.</font>

59
00:04:04,254 --> 00:04:11,492
<font color="#FFFFFF">The smoothed classifier is estimated with a hundred
thousand Monte Carlo samples at a one percent confidence level,</font>

60
00:04:11,572 --> 00:04:16,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">and every linear program is solved with the MOSEK
solver through CVXPY.</font>

61
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:19,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is a night-and-day improvement.</font>

62
00:04:19,616 --> 00:04:21,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">As the number of injected nodes grows,</font>

63
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:25,672
<font color="#FFFFFF">the sample-wise baseline collapses to zero
certified nodes,</font>

64
00:04:25,752 --> 00:04:28,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the collective certificates hold strong.</font>

65
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:31,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Citeseer with a hundred and forty injected nodes,</font>

66
00:04:31,951 --> 00:04:37,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">the standard collective relaxation certifies
seventy-three percent of targets and the</font>

67
00:04:37,327 --> 00:04:40,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">customized version reaches eighty-one point two percent,</font>

68
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:43,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">both against zero percent for sample-wise.</font>

69
00:04:43,526 --> 00:04:48,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">The customized Collective-LP-two consistently
matches or beats the standard one,</font>

70
00:04:48,587 --> 00:04:53,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">in one setting by over two hundred percent
relative, and it does so far faster,</font>

71
00:04:53,584 --> 00:04:56,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">solving even the largest budgets in about a minute.</font>

72
00:04:57,110 --> 00:05:01,033
<font color="#FFFFFF">How much does the linear relaxation cost us?</font>

73
00:05:01,113 --> 00:05:05,036
<font color="#FFFFFF">To find out,</font>

74
00:05:05,116 --> 00:05:09,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors compare the relaxed program against the
exact integer program,</font>

75
00:05:09,119 --> 00:05:13,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is only tractable for very small budgets.</font>

76
00:05:13,122 --> 00:05:17,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gap is small: the customized relaxation loses
only about five percent in certified ratio,</font>

77
00:05:17,125 --> 00:05:21,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">which also explains why the collective approach can
trail sample-wise at very tiny budgets.</font>

78
00:05:21,128 --> 00:05:25,051
<font color="#FFFFFF">On runtime,</font>

79
00:05:25,131 --> 00:05:29,054
<font color="#FFFFFF">the standard relaxation blows up past a thousand
seconds as the attack grows,</font>

80
00:05:29,134 --> 00:05:33,057
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the customized version stays near a single minute,</font>

81
00:05:33,137 --> 00:05:37,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">and at equal clean accuracy it dominates the
standard version on the accuracy-versus-certification trade-off.</font>

82
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,901
<font color="#FFFFFF">To put numbers on it: on Citeseer at a hundred and
forty injected nodes,</font>

83
00:05:41,981 --> 00:05:47,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">the certified ratio jumps from zero percent under
sample-wise certification to eighty-one</font>

84
00:05:47,595 --> 00:05:51,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">point two percent with the customized collective method.</font>

85
00:05:51,127 --> 00:05:54,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">That collective linear program solves in roughly
one minute.</font>

86
00:05:54,911 --> 00:06:01,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">The customized relaxation improves on the standard
one by up to two hundred and sixteen percent relative,</font>

87
00:06:01,534 --> 00:06:06,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it sits only about five percent below the exact
but far slower integer solution.</font>

88
00:06:06,832 --> 00:06:09,653
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short, injecting five percent of the graph,</font>

89
00:06:09,733 --> 00:06:14,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">the certified ratio climbs from zero to over eighty
percent on both datasets.</font>

90
00:06:14,890 --> 00:06:17,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lesson is simple but powerful.</font>

91
00:06:17,095 --> 00:06:21,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">Certifying a whole set of nodes together, instead
of one at a time,</font>

92
00:06:21,439 --> 00:06:27,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">transforms a near-useless zero percent guarantee
into an eighty percent certified ratio</font>

93
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:29,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">against graph injection attacks.</font>

94
00:06:29,155 --> 00:06:32,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">A customized linear relaxation keeps this tractable,</font>

95
00:06:32,527 --> 00:06:35,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">solving in about a minute even for large attacks.</font>

96
00:06:35,704 --> 00:06:39,904
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is a concrete step toward provable defenses that
are practical,</font>

97
00:06:39,984 --> 00:06:42,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">and because it shares the same smoothed model,</font>

98
00:06:42,966 --> 00:06:49,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">it plugs right in alongside existing sample-wise
certificates to stay strong across every attack budget.</font>
